-- title:
--   Galois descent, Hilbert 90 and norm for idèles
-- statement:
--   Let $E \subseteq F$ be number fields with $F/E$ Galois, and let $G = F \simeq_{\mathrm{alg}[E]} F$ denote the Galois group. Let $D$ be an element of `IdeleGaloisDescent (𝓞 F) E F`, that is, a monoid homomorphism $\mathrm{act}$ from $G$ to the ring automorphisms of the adèle ring $\mathbb{A}_F$ of $F$, such that $\mathrm{act}(g)$ carries $\mathrm{algebraMap}_F(x)$ to $\mathrm{algebraMap}_F(g x)$ for all $x \in F$ and each $\mathrm{act}(g)$ is continuous; $D.\mathrm{unitsAct}$ is the induced action of $G$ on the unit group $\mathbb{A}_F^\times$. Write $\beta \colon \mathbb{A}_E \to \mathbb{A}_F$ for the ring homomorphism underlying `genuineBaseChange E F`, which is compatible with the structure maps from $E$ and $F$ and exhibits $\mathbb{A}_F$ as $\mathbb{A}_E \otimes_E F$, and let $N \colon \mathbb{A}_F^\times \to \mathbb{A}_E^\times$ be the unit-group map induced by the algebra norm of $\mathbb{A}_F$ over $\mathbb{A}_E$ taken through $\beta$. Let $P \le \mathbb{A}_F^\times$ be the image of $F^\times$ under $\mathrm{algebraMap}_F$. Then four statements hold simultaneously: $\beta$ is injective; a unit $u \in \mathbb{A}_F^\times$ satisfies $D.\mathrm{unitsAct}(\tau)(u) = u$ for all $\tau \in G$ if and only if $u$ lies in the image of $\mathbb{A}_E^\times$ under $\beta$; if $(D.\mathrm{unitsAct}(\tau)(u))^{-1} u \in P$ for all $\tau \in G$, then $u$ lies in the subgroup generated by the image of $\mathbb{A}_E^\times$ together with $P$; and $\prod_{\tau \in G} D.\mathrm{unitsAct}(\tau)(u) = \beta(N(u))$ for every $u$.
--
--   These are the standard compatibilities between a Galois action on idèles and the idelic norm: descent $\mathbb{I}_F^{G} = \beta(\mathbb{I}_E)$, the idèle-class form of Hilbert's Theorem 90 (fixed classes in $\mathbb{I}_F/F^\times$ come from $E$, reflecting $H^1(G, F^\times) = 0$), and the identity expressing the norm of the Galois étale algebra $\mathbb{A}_E \otimes_E F$ as the product of the conjugates. The four assertions are packaged together because they are precisely the input hypotheses of the later index and norm-coset computations for idèle class groups, and the statement is quantified over an arbitrary descent datum $D$, of which there is at most one by [`M4aHerbrand.subsingleton_ideleGaloisDescent`](thm.html#M4aHerbrand.subsingleton_ideleGaloisDescent).
-- source:
